in{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A$\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A$\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247002 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1069f3ce-ee29-5917-b7ea-2041ea76b45b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247002 #edb/ref 1074 "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nFirst, we compute $A-I:$\n\n$$\nA-I=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-I:$\n\n$$\n(A-I)v=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A-I:$\n\n$$\n(A-I)w=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\